-- name: Files touched outside any declared plan
-- class: Consistency
-- metric: alignment_collect_d1
-- action: declare the touched files in a slice or plan, or confirm the out-of-plan work was deliberate scope
-- severity: WARN
-- guardrail: BOTH SIDES OF THIS DISAGREEMENT LIVE IN alignment-collect's OWN d1 RECORD -- spec docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-30-loopsmith-insight-data-platform-design.md:529's own parenthetical names d1 files_changed_outside_any_plan directly; alignment-collect.sh's own printf (lines 445-446) emits commits_with_source and files_changed_outside_any_plan together in one object, so no join is needed or possible. POPULATION IS SCOPED TO PACKS THAT ACTUALLY CARRY A d1 BLOCK THAT IS A WELL-FORMED JSON OBJECT (json_type(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1') = 'OBJECT'), NOT MERELY "THE KEY EXTRACTS TO SOMETHING NON-NULL" -- POST-PR-REVIEW BLOCKING FIX (PR #203): the prior guard, json_extract(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1') IS NOT NULL, was proven live to be a SECOND false-PASS trap, distinct from the schema-only one this design decision already fixed once: DuckDB's json_extract returns a non-SQL-NULL value for ANY present key regardless of JSON type -- a JSON null literal, an array, a bare string all satisfy IS NOT NULL; only a genuinely MISSING key returns SQL NULL. So that guard proved "the key exists", not "the key holds an object" -- a pack whose d1 is `null`, `[1,2,3]`, or a comma-joined string (e.g. files_changed_outside_any_plan: "scratch.py,notes.md" instead of a real array) read population 1 under it, while commits_with_source/files_changed_outside_any_plan then extracted NULL and were softened by COALESCE(...,0) into "nothing to flag", rendering a confident false PASS for a payload that was never actually shaped like d1 at all -- reproduced live against '{"dimensions":{"d1":null}}', '{"dimensions":{"d1":[1,2,3]}}', and a d1 object whose own files_changed_outside_any_plan was a bare string, all as {"severity":"PASS","evidence":[]} with population (1,) before this fix. FIXED by json_type(...) = 'OBJECT', mirroring coverage_review_missing.sql's own json_type(dp.config_json, '$.work.require_review') = 'BOOLEAN' idiom (that rule's own guardrail, same codebase, three files away) -- confirmed live: json_type returns 'OBJECT' for a real object, 'NULL' for a JSON null literal (still not SQL NULL), 'ARRAY', 'VARCHAR', and only Python None for a genuinely missing key, so this guard alone now separates all four. THE SAME json_type = 'OBJECT' CHECK IS ALSO ADDED TO THE EVIDENCE QUERY'S OWN WHERE CLAUSE, FOR DEFENSE IN DEPTH -- population and evidence are two independently-executed queries (evaluate.py runs the evidence query only when population > 0, but does not itself filter evidence rows by the population's own predicate), so a store mixing a well-formed pack with a malformed one would have population > 0 read correctly while the evidence query, unguarded, could still evaluate the malformed row's own COALESCE-softened NULLs; gating the evidence query on the same json_type check closes that gap directly rather than relying on the population check alone. A SECOND, NESTED json_type GUARD ON files_changed_outside_any_plan ITSELF (json_type(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1.files_changed_outside_any_plan') = 'ARRAY') -- a d1 THAT IS a well-formed OBJECT can still carry a WRONG-SHAPED files_changed_outside_any_plan sub-field (e.g. a comma-joined STRING instead of a real array), and the json_type(d1) = 'OBJECT' guard alone does not catch that, since d1 genuinely is an object in that case; reproduced live this session, post-PR-review, against '{"dimensions":{"d1":{"commits_with_source":5,"files_changed_outside_any_plan":"scratch.py,notes.md"}}}' -- population still read 1 and evidence still rendered a false PASS with the top-level guard alone, the exact same collapse one level deeper. THIS NESTED GUARD IS ADDED TO BOTH POPULATION AND EVIDENCE, not evidence alone, because a pack whose files_changed_outside_any_plan is unmeasurable is unmeasurable for THIS rule specifically, the same "can't measure it, don't count it as checked" posture the top-level d1 guard already established. NOTE FOR A FUTURE READER, MIRRORING THE SIBLING RULE'S OWN test_command_known NOTE: a d1 that is a well-formed object whose files_changed_outside_any_plan key is entirely ABSENT (rather than present-and-wrong-shaped) also renders ABSENT under this nested guard (json_type of a missing key is SQL NULL, and NULL = 'ARRAY' is NULL, not TRUE) -- unreachable through the real alignment-collect.sh writer today (it always emits files_changed_outside_any_plan whenever it emits d1 at all, per its own printf line 445), but disclosed here for the same reason. THE EVIDENCE ARRAY COLUMN IS A DIRECT CAST(... AS VARCHAR[]) OF THE JSON ARRAY -- proven live this session to return a native Python list through the DB-API with no UNNEST needed (Design decision 4), matching coverage_gate_absent.sql's/coverage_degraded_collector.sql's own array-evidence precedent for degraded_collector/degraded_adapter; this is new idiom for a raw_payload-derived array specifically (no existing metric or rule had ever surfaced one before this story -- 20.sql explodes its own churn_hotspots array via from_json/unnest instead of returning it directly). REQUIRES commits_with_source > 0 (an existence check, not a magnitude) SO A PACK WITH NO REAL SOURCE ACTIVITY NEVER FIRES ON A STALE OR EMPTY ARRAY -- COALESCE(json_array_length(...), 0) > 0 mirrors 30.sql's own COALESCE(len(...), 0) array-emptiness idiom, adapted to json_array_length because the value here is a JSON value extracted from raw_payload, not yet a native VARCHAR[] column. files_outside_plan_confidence IS CARRIED THROUGH AS AN HONEST, NON-GATING, INFORMATIONAL COLUMN -- alignment-collect.sh itself always emits "low" here today (its own printf line 445), never varying per pack, so this rule's WHERE clause never reads or gates on it (there is only one value it could ever be); it is surfaced purely so a consumer sees the upstream heuristic's own stated low confidence right in the evidence row (Design decision 6), the same passthrough posture coverage_gate_absent.sql already established for degraded_collector/degraded_adapter. WHY d1-SOURCED RATHER THAN A fact_slice JOIN -- fact_slice has a real writer (artifact_reader.py's write_slices) but no join key to any commit or collector-pack row: a slice's own files[] is a plan-time declaration with no timestamp or commit-sha correlation to d1's observed per-commit file list, and this repo has no .slices.json at all today; alignment-collect.sh already performs the file-vs-plan correlation this rule needs (its own found_in_plan logic, lines ~323-337), so this rule reads that already-computed result rather than re-deriving it against a disconnected table (Design decision 3's rejected alternative). MALFORMED-PAYLOAD DISCLOSURE, CORRECTED (post-PR-review, this line previously overclaimed): SYNTACTICALLY INVALID JSON (raw_payload is non-NULL text that will not parse as JSON at all) still makes every json_extract/json_type call in this rule's population and evidence queries raise DuckDB's InvalidInputException, crashing evaluate_rule rather than degrading to ABSENT -- the identical, unguarded json_extract(raw_payload, ...) pattern every one of the six already-shipped rules (and metrics 5/20/24/41) already shares; this fails LOUD, not silent, so it is not spec:534's forbidden collapse, and widening scope to guard every json_extract call across the catalog against syntactically-invalid JSON is out of this story's scope (see .sdlc/plans/120.md Risks). VALID JSON OF THE WRONG SHAPE (a d1 that parses fine but is null/array/scalar rather than an object, or whose own files_changed_outside_any_plan is present but not a real array) is a SEPARATE case, and -- unlike the syntactically-invalid case -- IS now caught, not merely disclosed: the json_type(...) = 'OBJECT' guard on d1 itself, PLUS the nested json_type(...) = 'ARRAY' guard on files_changed_outside_any_plan, together exclude every wrong-shaped variant from both population and evidence, rendering the honest ABSENT rather than crashing or silently passing. ORDER BY IS A TOTAL ORDER, UNLIKE coverage_degraded_collector.sql'S OWN PRECEDENT (non-blocking, flagged in review) -- fact_collector_pack has no primary key and collected_ts carries no uniqueness guarantee (store.py's DDL, re-checked), so two packs for one project written in the same instant would otherwise leave residual engine-whim ordering between them, the same accepted limitation 30.sql's own guardrail already names for an analogous LAG tiebreaker; here, `rowid` (DuckDB's own per-row identity, confirmed live to be queryable and distinct per row on both an in-memory and a file-backed connection) is appended as the final sort key, making this rule's own evidence order fully deterministic without touching the pre-existing catalog's shared, disclosed limitation.
-- population: SELECT count(*) FROM fact_collector_pack WHERE schema = 'alignment-collect/v1' AND json_type(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1') = 'OBJECT' AND json_type(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1.files_changed_outside_any_plan') = 'ARRAY'
SELECT
    project_id,
    collected_ts,
    CAST(json_extract(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1.commits_with_source') AS INTEGER) AS commits_with_source,
    CAST(json_extract(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1.files_changed_outside_any_plan') AS VARCHAR[]) AS files_changed_outside_any_plan,
    json_extract_string(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1.files_outside_plan_confidence') AS files_outside_plan_confidence
FROM fact_collector_pack
WHERE schema = 'alignment-collect/v1'
  AND json_type(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1') = 'OBJECT'
  AND json_type(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1.files_changed_outside_any_plan') = 'ARRAY'
  AND COALESCE(CAST(json_extract(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1.commits_with_source') AS INTEGER), 0) > 0
  AND COALESCE(json_array_length(json_extract(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1.files_changed_outside_any_plan')), 0) > 0
ORDER BY project_id, collected_ts, rowid
